WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.989
Film Courage: How much time today in 2018
do you dedicate to writing?

00:00:03.989 --> 00:00:08.090
Gordy Hoffman, Screenwriter/Founder of Blue
Cat Screenplay Competition:

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:15.020
I don’t write in a disciplined way in a
timing thing a certain hour of the day, every

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.859
single day, Monday through Friday or anything
like that.

00:00:16.859 --> 00:00:21.442
I’m one of those guys who writes in bunches
and writes at certain times of the day.

00:00:21.442 --> 00:00:22.442
I’m one of those guys who writes in bunches
or writes in certain times.

00:00:22.442 --> 00:00:23.442
There are certain days that I don’t write.

00:00:23.442 --> 00:00:26.230
I wrote five pages this morning.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:30.619
I did not do that for the benefit of the Film
Courage interview.

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:38.510
I was just like yesterday I need to, I’m
rewriting this thing and I’ve been going

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:45.650
around and around with it and I’ve started
really different approach on this script.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:53.610
There are two projects that I’m working
on right now both of them to direct and both

00:00:53.610 --> 00:01:00.460
of them have gone through a lot of different
development and there are people involved,

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:01.970
producers and stuff.

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:12.370
And I want to make one of them next year so
I really need to…I found myself bored…getting

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:18.720
really frustrated with this one thing and
I’m at this point where I don’t know if

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.300
I can figure this thing out and I was like
“You’ve got to hang in there.”

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:27.800
And I did and then I came in for a way to
open it again.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.760
So I wrote a page on Friday.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.280
I had this idea for the opening so I wrote
a page.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.141
And then today I wrote four more pages so
I’m on page 5 and then I sent it over to

00:01:40.141 --> 00:01:46.140
my staff (I send my pages in) and they kind
of take a look at them and give me quick feedback

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.280
to make sure I’m going in the right direction
or something like that.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.260
And then this afternoon I’m going to go
to the other project where I’m outlining

00:01:53.260 --> 00:02:00.990
it because it’s a heist and so I have to
do a lot of architecture stuff…It’s like

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:08.459
“Aarghh” Whereas this other thing is very
much like a LOVE LIZA where there’s a protagonist

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:13.540
and they’re on a mission kind of thing and
it’s that kind of a piece.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:21.080
So it’s very much an extension of LOVE LIZA
but in that structurally, that character arc

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.670
kind of thing but I’ going to be directing
it.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:30.260
Film Courage: You mentioned getting bored
and so when you get that signal that nothing

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:32.420
is happening, what’s your process?

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.900
Gordy: You cannot abandon it.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:41.209
Every script…if you get bored with it, that
means you’re actually getting somewhere

00:02:41.209 --> 00:02:42.770
with the movie.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:49.349
People are amateurs primarily because they
can’t get through the professional period

00:02:49.349 --> 00:02:55.380
of hating what they’re writing, bored, frustrated,
looking at every page going “I hate all

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.380
these scenes.”

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:57.680
I’m like “Ugh!”

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.660
There’s no magic here, I’m not enjoying
myself, it’s not gratifying.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:07.940
So you have to suck it up and get through
that period, you can’t go…”Oh, I’m

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:08.940
bored.”

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.230
You are just going to go right back to where
that point is again.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:18.400
There is a famous quote from Kurosawa about
this that I look at this almost every single

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:25.500
day (the quote) you should put it up with
this video because people will probably ask

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:31.410
about what it is but he talks about every
script falling into despair and he uses the

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:36.370
word despair and despair is a strong word
but it’s exactly what that is.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:42.690
It’s like I can’t figure this out, I don’t
know how to figure this out, and I really

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:47.599
want to start with this because this other
thing, I know what to do with it, I’m going

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:53.910
to start it, not knowing or not remembering
that everything you write, you come to that

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.849
place of despair.

00:03:56.849 --> 00:04:03.879
The artist, the writer, the director, the
actor (whatever) has to walk through that

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:12.180
period of desert period of the creative process
or they are not going to…or something will

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.180
click.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:17.280
And that’s what happened is I hung in there
with this one thing where I was like “Oh

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.789
my God,” and then I came up with an image
and then I got some input from somebody (just

00:04:22.789 --> 00:04:28.710
life experience) and I had some incident and
I was like “What if I move it and I started

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:29.710
over there?

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:31.199
And I do that.”

00:04:31.199 --> 00:04:36.069
And then all the sudden I had an idea and
I was like “This is how it is.”

00:04:36.069 --> 00:04:41.580
And I cracked my problem and I was like “Okay,
I’m back in the saddle.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:42.779
I’ve got this thing going.

00:04:42.779 --> 00:04:48.499
Now I can incorporate the other things I’ve
been working on that I like but I got through

00:04:48.499 --> 00:04:50.330
that period of discouragement.

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:58.520
Film Courage: So when you know you’re in
that boredom, that you have a ritual for?

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.479
Gordy: It’s not a ritual.

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:04.169
A ritual is staying patient.

00:05:04.169 --> 00:05:08.050
People don’t have any patience.

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:12.310
Corporate structures I think don’t allow
for patience when they’re making studio

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:14.900
pictures and stuff.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.520
When they announce a release date and the
script is not done, well that’s counter

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:25.319
intuitive actually because it’s like “I’m
going to show you this but I haven’t written

00:05:25.319 --> 00:05:26.319
it yet.”

00:05:26.319 --> 00:05:27.849
That creates dysfunction.

00:05:27.849 --> 00:05:32.369
So that’s why I think a lot of bets are
off and that’s why you have projects not

00:05:32.369 --> 00:05:33.970
really work out.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:38.869
You go to a movie, you go to the theater and
you’re like “What just happened in there?”

00:05:38.869 --> 00:05:44.270
And it’s very difficult because it really
eliminates the principle of patience because

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:49.960
you’re not going to be like Well, let’s
pump the breaks.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:53.240
This thing needs like 6 more months, I’ve
got to figure this out.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:58.979
Can we move this release date back for a year
and a half?

00:05:58.979 --> 00:06:02.869
And tell the Disney stockholders that the
STAR WARS movie is not going to come out for

00:06:02.869 --> 00:06:06.509
4 years because we want to make sure the script
is good.

00:06:06.509 --> 00:06:11.050
That’s not going to work and that’s okay.

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:15.740
It doesn’t mean that you can’t…I mean
the guys who wrote CASABLANCA, you can write

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:19.460
under duress and it can be successful.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:22.860
Having deadlines and structure is fine.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:29.009
But overall when you are stuck, having patience
and being like “Do not give up.

00:06:29.009 --> 00:06:30.009
Do not relent.

00:06:30.009 --> 00:06:31.749
Hang in there.”

00:06:31.749 --> 00:06:37.129
And don’t change gears and I did and it
was a couple of weeks and it was a little

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:42.919
bit of time and I was kind of like “No,
no, no…Just keep hanging in there.”

00:06:42.919 --> 00:06:48.080
And I was just like “What can you do here?

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:49.499
You have to hang in there.”

00:06:49.499 --> 00:06:53.879
There is noting platable about working on
this script at all.

00:06:53.879 --> 00:06:55.379
You want to start working on something else.

00:06:55.379 --> 00:07:01.699
There are so many ideas that are so much sexier
or attractive that you want to jump into but

00:07:01.699 --> 00:07:04.789
it’s like you can’t let go of this.

00:07:04.789 --> 00:07:08.159
You have to hang in there.

00:07:08.159 --> 00:07:13.809
So whatever the process is doesn’t matter,
it’s just exhibiting patience and realizing

00:07:13.809 --> 00:07:18.139
if you hang in there the path is going to
open up according to Kurosawa and then the

00:07:18.139 --> 00:07:22.749
path will open up and you’ll go “Ahh!”

00:07:22.749 --> 00:07:26.460
And a lightbulb will go off and that’s what’s
happened with everything really.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:30.940
Film Courage: APOCALYPSE NOW you don’t want
it to become your HEART OF DARKNESS.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:32.969
Did you see the documentary?

00:07:32.969 --> 00:07:33.969
It’s excellent.

00:07:33.969 --> 00:07:45.469
Gordy: Yes I did but I think if anything what
Coppola was doing with APOCALYPSE NOW was

00:07:45.469 --> 00:07:51.819
having patience and hanging in there because
a lot of places you want to give up but you

00:07:51.819 --> 00:07:55.969
have to fight, a lot of places where you have
to fight through and maintaining that you

00:07:55.969 --> 00:08:00.949
have to tell the truth, I want to be original,
I want to be compelling, I want to make imaginative

00:08:00.949 --> 00:08:09.039
choices because to get through the darkness
you’re bored or your stuck because you’re

00:08:09.039 --> 00:08:12.209
not giving up and you’re not compromising.

00:08:12.209 --> 00:08:16.240
You’re not saying “Well, let’s just
shoot it anyways.

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:18.160
It’s good enough.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.520
It’s already done.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:22.559
Let’s just do it.

00:08:22.559 --> 00:08:23.559
It’s good enough.

00:08:23.559 --> 00:08:26.059
It doesn’t have to be perfect.”

00:08:26.059 --> 00:08:29.999
I had a filmmaker say to me once “Gordy,
it doesn’t have to be perfect.”

00:08:29.999 --> 00:08:32.669
And it’s like “You’re right.

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:35.300
It’s never going to be perfect.”

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:42.780
But what it’s implying is a little bit of
disregard of “Who cares?”

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:50.460
If you know something is kind of crap, or
it’s not believable or it’s not logical

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:54.760
or it’s not vulnerable or it isn’t clear
or whatever.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:09:03.660
If you’re aware of problems but you’re
like “Well, who cares?

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:08.030
Then you’re not going to make someone’s
favorite movie that way.”

